    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit rule as a new guard step in the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline, following the existing capacity-guard pattern (`RequireCityCapacity`).\n\n**New files:**\n- `RequireDailyCapacity.java` \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today; throws `DailyLimitExceededException` when the count reaches 100. Runs before `BuildOwner`, so nothing is persisted on rejection.\n- `DailyLimitExceededException.java` \u2014 checked exception (so it appears in the `throws` clause).\n- `DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `429 Too Many Requests`.\n- `org.springframework...DailyLimitExceededException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `requireDailyCapacity` step (governed by `transaction`) between `requireCityCapacity` and `build`.\n\nSince new owners default their `registrationDate` to today (`ApplyRegistrationDate`), this correctly caps registrations at 100 per day and rejects the 101st with a 429, matching the acceptance test.",
